gradients: [('The prompt may have gotten these examples wrong due to its focus on the general truthfulness of the statement without adequately emphasizing the need to evaluate the specific claims made within each context. Additionally, the prompt may not be guiding the classifier to consider the nuances in the claims that could lead to misinterpretation or misrepresentation, such as the distinction between factual accuracy and opinion-based statements. This lack of specificity could result in a misjudgment of the truthfulness of statements that are, in fact, misleading or false despite being presented in a seemingly credible manner. Furthermore, the prompt may not account for the importance of context and the implications of the statements made, leading to a misalignment in expectations for evaluating truthfulness.', '## Example 1\nText: "Statement: I was able to go and buy an automatic weapon Most people can go out and buy an automatic weapon.\nJob title: CNN anchor\nState: Georgia\nParty: journalist\nContext: a live broadcast on CNN"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 2\nText: "Statement: OShaughnessy even voted for her own personal stimulus package -- a taxpayer-funded pay raise for herself.\nJob title: Ohio secretary of state\nState: Ohio\nParty: republican\nContext: a television campaign ad"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 3\nText: "Statement: Today there are more men and women out of work in America than there are people working in Canada.\nJob title: Former governor\nState: Massachusetts\nParty: republican\nContext: a speech to the Conservative Political Action Conference"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 4\nText: "Statement: A cashier earning $20,000 per year pays the same (Virginia) income tax rate as a hedge fund manager earning $20 million per year.\nJob title: Political consultant\nState: Virginia\nParty: democrat\nContext: a news release."\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No')]
